1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,870
In 1998, Ocarina of Time asked you to save
Hyrule.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,770
Almost three decades later, Nintendo is
asking you to go back.

3
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:13,350
New graphics. An orchestra. Voices. Modern
controls. A new camera.

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:14,730
The same ocarina.

5
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:15,850
The same sword.

6
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,310
The same Triforce.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,970
But there is one thing Nintendo cannot
rebuild from the ground up.

8
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:21,730
You.

9
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:25,690
And somehow, that might be exactly why we
want to go back.

10
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,360
So, why?

11
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:33,239
Because the Ocarina of Time people
remember is not exactly the game that came

12
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:34,370
on the cartridge.

13
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:35,290
It is the game...

14
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,270
plus the room.

15
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:38,670
Plus the television.

16
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,590
Plus the friend who somehow knew where to
go.

17
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:46,059
Plus an entire Saturday afternoon where
nobody could reach you because smartphones

18
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:49,190
had not yet ruined that particular
miracle.

19
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:53,270
For some players, walking into Hyrule
Field felt enormous.

20
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:55,030
Not because it actually was.

21
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:56,870
Because you were smaller.

22
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:00,550
And memory is very good at preserving
feelings...

23
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:05,400
while being absolutely terrible at
preserving technical specifications.

24
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,230
Nobody wakes up thinking:

25
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,650
Man... I really miss Nintendo 64 texture
filtering.

26
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:13,290
You remember the forest.

27
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:14,270
The music.

28
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,130
The castle in the distance.

29
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:21,370
That strange feeling that if you just kept
walking, the world might continue forever.

30
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:22,890
Which creates a problem.

31
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:25,590
Because Nintendo can remake Ocarina of
Time.

32
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:27,750
But part of the thing people want back...

33
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,080
was never inside Ocarina of Time.

34
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:35,090
The new version is being rebuilt for
Switch 2.

35
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,070
New visuals.

36
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:37,290
Voiced cutscenes.

37
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:38,710
Expanded dialogue.

38
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:40,270
An orchestral score.

39
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,890
Modernized controls and camera movement.

40
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:45,510
On paper, the job sounds easy.

41
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:46,410
Take something beloved...

42
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,670
and make everything better.

43
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:52,010
Except that is where remakes become
dangerous.

44
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:54,070
Because better is measurable.

45
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,290
More polygons.

46
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:56,230
Better lighting.

47
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:57,290
Better animation.

48
00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:58,390
Better sound.

49
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:00,870
But familiar is not measurable.

50
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:04,900
An empty field in 1998 could feel
enormous.

51
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:07,210
Rebuild it too literally today...

52
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:09,270
and it might just feel empty.

53
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:12,620
A silent character once left space for
your imagination.

54
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,250
Give that character a voice...

55
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,910
and suddenly somebody has to decide what
that silence sounded like.

56
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,410
A camera you once fought with was part of
learning the game.

57
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:22,010
Fix it...

58
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:24,350
and the game becomes easier to inhabit.

59
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:25,430
Which is good.

60
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:26,330
Probably.

61
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,850
But every improvement quietly changes the
memory attached to it.

62
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,110
That is the impossible job.

63
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:34,830
Make it different enough to justify
existing...

64
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,000
without making people wonder where their
game went.

65
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,650
And Nintendo has another problem.

66
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:44,030
This remake is not really being made for
one audience.

67
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:46,020
It is being made for two.

68
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:48,190
Player one already knows everything.

69
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:50,130
They know what that ocarina means.

70
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:52,850
They know what happens when Link pulls the
Master Sword.

71
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,419
They see three golden triangles and
immediately understand that somebody is

72
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:58,670
about to make a very bad decision.

73
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,179
The Triforce represents wisdom, power and
courage,

74
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,720
and Link’s journey spans Hyrule across two
different eras.

75
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:06,730
Player two?

76
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:08,870
Player two knows none of this.

77
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:11,010
They meet the Great Deku Tree and think:

78
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:14,890
That is an extremely large tree with an
extremely personal problem.

79
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,680
For them, Hyrule has no nostalgia.

80
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,490
No childhood attached to it.

81
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:22,010
No twenty-eight years of expectations.

82
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:25,150
So the remake has two completely different
jobs.

83
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,370
For the new player:

84
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,090
Make a game from 1998 feel natural in
2026.

85
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:31,550
For the returning player:

86
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,090
Make something they already know...

87
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:35,410
feel like discovery again.

88
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:37,330
One person is looking at Hyrule.

89
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:40,050
The other is looking for the Hyrule in
their head.

90
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:43,360
And somehow Nintendo has to build both.

91
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:48,440
The safest remake imaginable would change
almost nothing.

92
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:49,650
Sharper textures.

93
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:50,910
Better resolution.

94
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:52,150
Cleaner controls.

95
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:54,850
Everything exactly where you remember it.

96
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,570
And that sounds respectful.

97
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,000
But there is a problem.

98
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:01,370
Ocarina of Time was not important because
it felt familiar.

99
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:03,790
It was important because it felt new.

100
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:07,639
A world people understood in two
dimensions suddenly became somewhere they

101
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:08,870
could stand.

102
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:10,150
Distance mattered.

103
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,070
Where you looked mattered.

104
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:13,490
Music mattered.

105
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:14,860
And most importantly...

106
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:16,750
time mattered.

107
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,830
You were a child.

108
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:19,310
Then you were not.

109
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,470
You disappeared...

110
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:22,510
and the world changed without you.

111
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:25,550
That is not some side detail of Ocarina of
Time.

112
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:27,450
That is the game.

113
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:31,690
Link’s adventure is a journey through
Hyrule across two distinct eras.

114
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:34,870
So maybe changing Ocarina of Time is not
disrespecting it.

115
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:38,250
Maybe refusing to change anything would
be.

116
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,420
Because the goal should not be to preserve
every limitation from 1998.

117
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:44,350
The goal should be to understand...

118
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:47,310
what those limitations made you feel.

119
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:51,160
And then find a way to make someone feel
that again.

120
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:54,510
Which brings us back to the strange part.

121
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:56,850
The original game still exists.

122
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,370
We did not lose it.

123
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:02,490
Nobody needs Nintendo to rescue Ocarina of
Time from history.

124
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:04,610
So why do we want it again?

125
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:09,819
Because going back to something you loved
is one of the few ways you can measure how

126
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:11,250
much you changed.

127
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:13,370
The forest is still there.

128
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:15,410
The ocarina is still there.

129
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:17,650
The Master Sword is still waiting.

130
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:21,930
The Triforce still means wisdom, power and
courage.

131
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:25,830
But the person holding the controller is
different.

132
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:28,030
And for this particular game...

133
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:30,530
that is almost too perfect.

134
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:33,370
A story about a child becoming an adult...

135
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:37,310
is coming back to people who did exactly
that.

136
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:43,419
Somebody who first crossed Hyrule Field in
1998 can now cross it again with almost

137
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:46,670
three decades between those two versions
of themselves.

138
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:48,580
Same road.

139
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:49,970
Different person.

140
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:52,730
And maybe that is what we actually want.

141
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:54,750
Not the old game back.

142
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:57,310
A chance to stand beside an old memory...

143
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:01,220
and see if it still recognizes us.

144
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:02,430
So...

145
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:04,840
can you remake a memory?

146
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:06,080
Probably not.

147
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,730
Nintendo cannot rebuild the room you were
sitting in.

148
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,350
It cannot recreate the television.

149
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,310
The Saturday afternoon.

150
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,210
The friend sitting next to you.

151
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:19,110
Or the exact version of you who walked
into Hyrule for the first time.

152
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:21,230
But it can rebuild Hyrule.

153
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,590
And maybe that is enough.

154
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:25,670
Because a remake does not have to take you
back.

155
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:27,810
It can let two different moments meet.

156
00:06:27,860 --> 00:06:29,860
The game you remember...

157
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,980
and the person you became.

158
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,450
You cannot play Ocarina of Time for the
first time again.

159
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:37,570
But maybe you can meet it again.

160
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:38,670
And this time...

161
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:40,560
you both grew up.

162
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,290
So what game would you go back to?

163
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:48,080
And what game deserves a “why?” next?

164
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:51,620
Tell me in the comments. Maybe that’s our
next quest.
